From b2b0724c65ed3f2e45c0261b7e96b3e3d0419609 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: cruz <cruz@tbu.net>
Date: Thu, 20 Aug 2026 10:52:30 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 019/206] test(shorebird): static_assert WrapperAllocator is
 0x98

Machine-checks the DWARF byte size that was previously only measured by hand.
A compile-time assert needs no access to the private fields -- the offsetof
version does not compile -- costs nothing at runtime, and breaks the build the
moment the layout drifts.

Verified it bites: changing 0x98 to 0x99 fails the build with
"static assertion failed due to requirement 'sizeof(...) == 153'", ninja
exit 1. Restored to 0x98, builds clean.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>

diff --git a/runtime/vm/shorebird/wrapper.cc b/runtime/vm/shorebird/wrapper.cc
index 827c5ec610e..6cdab1a0904 100644
--- a/runtime/vm/shorebird/wrapper.cc
+++ b/runtime/vm/shorebird/wrapper.cc
@@ -7,6 +7,14 @@
 namespace dart {
 namespace shorebird {
 
+// Their DW_AT_byte_size for WrapperAllocator. A compile-time assert needs no
+// access to the private fields (which is why the offsetof version won't
+// compile), costs nothing at runtime, and breaks the build the moment the
+// layout drifts. Field *ordering* is covered more meaningfully by the
+// round-trip arithmetic tests than an offset check would be.
+static_assert(sizeof(WrapperAllocator) == 0x98,
+              "WrapperAllocator must match Shorebird's 0x98 layout");
+
 // The real implementations resolve the generated stub's address range. The
 // stubs do not exist until plan #2, so these report an empty range for now.
 // Task 3 tests assert that an empty range is rejected rather than remapped.
